VT_TALK("sql/sql-recursive", {
 "basics": "Some questions have no fixed number of steps, like everyone who reports to Priya, directly or through others. A recursive CTE answers them. You write WITH RECURSIVE, a name, and two queries joined by UNION ALL. The first is the anchor, which runs once and makes the starting rows. The second is the recursive part, which reads the CTE's own name and sees only the rows found in the round before. Round after round, new rows appear, until a round finds nothing. In the animation, count to a number of your choice, or walk down the org chart, and watch each round add its rows. Always include a stop rule. Think of a chain of phone calls where each person rings the next.",
 "series": "A recursive CTE is also a factory for numbers and dates. Start with the first of June, and each round add one day, until you reach the tenth. Then left join the orders, and every day appears in the report, even quiet days with zero orders, which a plain GROUP BY would leave out. A CTE can carry several columns from round to round, such as a counter and a running power of two. PostgreSQL and newer SQL Server have a built in generate series function, and elsewhere the recursive CTE does the job. Think of a desk calendar where every page is there, even for days with no appointments.",
 "tree": "When a table points at itself, such as an employee's manager id pointing at another employee, it stores a tree. A recursive CTE walks that tree one level per round. The anchor starts at the top, the person with no manager, and each round joins the employees whose manager was found in the last round. Carry a level number and a path of names along, and you can draw the whole org chart with indents. Walk the other way, following the manager id upwards, and you get anyone's chain of bosses. The same pattern serves shop category menus, comment threads and folders. Think of a family tree traced down from the grandparents.",
 "graph": "A tree has one parent per node, but a graph can have many links and even loops, like train routes that come back to where they started. A naive recursive walk around a loop never ends, so you must guard against it. The common trick is to carry the path as text and skip any city that is already in it. UNION instead of UNION ALL also ends simple loops, because it refuses a row it has already produced. A depth limit or an outer LIMIT are extra safety nets, and PostgreSQL has a CYCLE clause that does the bookkeeping for you. Think of a traveller who ticks off each city on a map so they never go round in circles.",
 "levels": "Up the ladder we go. For a beginner, a recursive query keeps finding new rows from the rows it just found. At college level, it is an anchor joined to a recursive part by UNION ALL, with a stop rule, used for series, trees and graphs. At masters level, the engine runs semi naive evaluation, joining only the newest rows each round, and the answer is a least fixed point. At research level, these ideas come from Datalog, and new standards such as GQL and SQL property graph queries bring graph matching into databases. Think of ripples spreading from a stone dropped in a pond."
});
